---
import Photo from './Photo.astro';
import CtaPill from './CtaPill.astro';
import { hero } from '../data/frames';
import { business, contact, cta, heroGreeting, heroLede, heroStrip } from '../data/site';

/*
	The cover, rebuilt as a panel with a doorway in it.

	STRUCTURE comes from https://openhousecafe.in/ — a two-column typographic
	panel (headline, lede, one CTA left; photograph floating right), the
	photograph overhanging the panel's edge, and a three-slot info strip
	beneath it. SKIN comes from this project: canyon-ember ground, Fraunces on
	the --text-hero clamp, the amber pill this site already uses everywhere,
	the double-bezel shell every other photograph already sits in.

	THE DOORWAY is the one place this stops being a rectangle. The plate is
	the entrance sign — a photograph of an arch — and Photo.astro draws its
	shell as an arch to match it, so the frame and its treatment are the same
	idea: you are looking at the doorway, through a doorway. The box underneath
	is unchanged: 4:5, the plate's own ratio, so `cover` still removes nothing
	and the burnt-in caption along the bottom stays whole.

	What is NOT here any more: the full-bleed background, the two scrims, the
	svh budget arithmetic, and the fact rail. All four existed to solve one
	problem — an overlaying headline could not be allowed to collide with the
	burnt-in caption on the plate. Nothing overlays the photograph now, so the
	problem is gone and so is the machinery.

	THE OVERHANG is one negative margin, not absolute positioning: at lg the
	plate carries `margin-bottom: -3rem`, which shortens its own grid row by
	exactly that much (a grid item is centred on its margin box, so the box
	ends up that far past the row). The grid keeps 4rem of bottom padding —
	1rem of ember under the doorway, completed by the strip's 1.5rem and
	the dip correction into the vertical padding-equality identity (see the
	lg block). Because 4rem > 3rem the plate stays inside the panel at
	every viewport height. Below lg the plate is in normal flow, so the
	overhang is switched off.

	BREAKPOINTS: two columns only from lg up. At md the shell is ~688px wide
	and a 20rem plate leaves the lede 288px — about seven lines of 17px type,
	which is not a column, it is a gutter. Stacked until then, centred, the way
	the reference stacks its own.

	Verified by arithmetic, not by screenshot: no browser pass was run, per
	AGENTS.md.
*/
---

<section id="top">
	<div class="hero-panel">
		<div class="shell hero-grid">
			{/* One-time entrance, not a scroll reveal: the visitor has already
				landed, and arrival is the greeting. CSS keyframes on the copy's
				elements (not the container — the container carries its own
				nudge transform on phones) run exactly once, on load, in a set
				sequence: greeting, lede, action, then the doorway a beat
				later. `prefers-reduced-motion` gets the finished state. */}
			<div class="hero-copy">
		{/* Phone: the whole greeting on one line (see .hero-title). Desktop:
			the two spans stack (see the lg block) back into the original
			"Welcome to" / "Canyon!" break. */}
			<h1 class="hero-title hero-arrive" style="--hero-delay: 0ms">
				<span class="hero-line">{heroGreeting.line1}</span> <span class="hero-line">{heroGreeting.line2}</span>
			</h1>

				{/* [bio] verbatim, then facts assembled from this file — see site.ts. */}
				<p class="hero-lede hero-arrive mt-3 max-w-[34rem] text-pretty text-canyon-champagne/85 lg:mt-5" style="--hero-delay: 120ms">
					{heroLede.line1}<br />{heroLede.line2}
				</p>

				{/*
					One action: the telephone number has left the hero because it is now
					the third slot of the strip directly beneath this.
				*/}
				<div class="hero-arrive" style="--hero-delay: 240ms">
					<CtaPill label={cta.hero} variant="simple" class="mt-6 lg:mt-8" />
				</div>
			</div>

			{/*
				The doorway. The entrance sign, whole at its own 4:5, in the
				double bezel every other photograph on the site uses — outlined
				as an arch to answer the arch in the photograph itself.
				`priority` because this is the LCP candidate; `sizes` because the
				frame is one grid column wide, not the full-width gallery frame
				Photo.astro's default assumes.

				The glow behind it is the candlelight the arch wants: a soft oval
				of amber that breathes — 9 seconds, alternate, a few percent of
				its own opacity outward and back — slow enough to read as
				lamplight rather than as animation.
			*/}
			<div class="hero-plate">
				<div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
				<div class="hero-arrive" style="--hero-delay: 200ms">
					<Photo
						src={hero.src}
						alt={hero.alt}
						ratio={hero.ratio}
						arch
						priority
						widths={[320, 480, 640, 800, 960]}
						sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 25rem, min(84vw, 18rem)"
					/>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	{/*
		The strip. A <ul>, not headings and not a <dl>: these are
		label/value pairs, and a heading here would either skip from h1
		to h3 or push three h2s onto the front of the page before the
		Nights section has had its say. A <dl> would want dt/dd as
		direct children of each wrapper; the icon + stacked text layout
		does not give it that, so a plain list with labelled text keeps
		the markup valid.

		The middle slot is "The Nights", not "Open Hours" — closing time has
		never been published, and DESIGN.md §8 refuses to invent it. The slot
		carries the time-fact Canyon does publish instead.
	*/}
	<div class="shell info-strip">
		<ul class="strip">
		<li class="strip-item" data-reveal>
			<span class="strip-chip" aria-hidden="true">
				<svg
					class="h-5 w-5"
					viewBox="0 0 24 24"
					fill="none"
					stroke="currentColor"
					stroke-width="1.5"
					stroke-linecap="round"
					stroke-linejoin="round"
				>
					<path
						d="M20 10c0 4.99-5.54 10.19-7.4 11.8a1 1 0 0 1-1.2 0C9.54 20.19 4 14.99 4 10a8 8 0 0 1 16 0"
					></path>
					<circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3"></circle>
				</svg>
			</span>
			<div class="strip-text">
				<p class="strip-title">
					<span>{heroStrip.locate}</span>
				</p>
				<p class="strip-value">
					{business.address.line1}<br />{business.address.line2}
				</p>
			</div>
		</li>

		<li class="strip-item" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 80ms">
			<span class="strip-chip" aria-hidden="true">
				<svg
					class="h-5 w-5"
					viewBox="0 0 24 24"
					fill="none"
					stroke="currentColor"
					stroke-width="1.5"
					stroke-linecap="round"
					stroke-linejoin="round"
				>
					<path d="M9 18V5l12-2v13"></path>
					<circle cx="6" cy="18" r="3"></circle>
					<circle cx="18" cy="16" r="3"></circle>
				</svg>
			</span>
			<div class="strip-text">
				<p class="strip-title">
					<span>{heroStrip.nights}</span>
				</p>
				<p class="strip-value">Sufi · Bollywood · Strings, {business.eventStart}</p>
			</div>
		</li>

		<li class="strip-item" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 160ms">
			<span class="strip-chip" aria-hidden="true">
				<svg
					class="h-5 w-5"
					viewBox="0 0 24 24"
					fill="none"
					stroke="currentColor"
					stroke-width="1.5"
					stroke-linecap="round"
					stroke-linejoin="round"
				>
					<path
						d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1 1 .4 1.9.7 2.8a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8.1 9.9a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.2a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.5c.9.3 1.8.6 2.8.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2Z"
					></path>
				</svg>
			</span>
			<div class="strip-text">
				<p class="strip-title">
					<span>{heroStrip.reservation}</span>
				</p>
				<p class="strip-value">
					<a href={`tel:${contact.tel}`} class="strip-number">{contact.telDisplay}</a>
				</p>
			</div>
		</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	/*
		Single-viewport opening on desktop (the sticky bar above spends its
		own measured ~4.85rem band — see the lg block — so the column height
		accounts for it): panel + strip share that column. The panel grows to
		fill the leftover space and centres its content in it, so the strip
		sits pinned to the bottom of the viewport with a landing — Locate /
		Nights / Reservation end the first screen. Below lg the stack flows
		past the fold — copy, plate and three strip slots cannot legibly fit
		a phone screen.
	*/
	#top {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
	}

	@media (width >= 64rem) {
		#top {
			/* The bar is 77px tall in practice: the lockup logo is 390×172
				shown w-28 (49px) inside py-3.5 (28px). Reserved as a flat
				5rem so the strip's landing survives font drift either way. */
			min-height: calc(100svh - 5rem);
			justify-content: flex-start;
		}
	}

	/*
		The panel. One surface step above the page (ember on void — DESIGN.md's
		definition of ember, "raised surfaces"), with the ochre wall glow
		anchored at 78% 18% so it falls behind the photograph instead of
		behind the copy.
	*/
	.hero-panel {
		background-color: var(--color-canyon-ember);
		background-image: radial-gradient(
			70rem 45rem at 78% 18%,
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-canyon-ochre) 16%, transparent),
			transparent 62%
		);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-canyon-coal);
	}

	/*
		Stacked and centred by default — copy and plate as one phone screen;
		two columns from lg.

		The header sits in normal flow directly above on every size — sticky
		never overlay, one pixel-truth (see Nav.astro) — so the grid's
		padding-top is simply the rhythm beneath it: 1.25rem on phones,
		2.5rem at lg. Phones keep the full-viewport shape
		(`min-height: 100svh` with centred content), so the greeting and
		the doorway land as one screen the way the reference stacks its
		own. `min-height`, not `height`: on a short phone the content
		still grows past the fold instead of clipping. At lg the grid
		carries 4rem of padding-bottom: with the plate's -3rem overhang
		that leaves 1rem of ember under the doorway, and together with
		the strip's fixed 2.5rem it completes the padding-equality
		identity documented in the lg block below.
	*/
	.hero-grid {
		display: grid;
		gap: clamp(1.75rem, 5svh, 3.5rem);
		align-items: center;
		justify-items: center;
		min-height: 100svh;
		align-content: center;
		padding-top: 1.25rem;
		/* 30px between the photo and the panel's dividing line on phones.
			Desktop keeps 2rem in the lg block: the overhang math spends it. */
		padding-bottom: 30px;
	}

	/* Centred stack: badge, greeting, lede and CTA all on the middle line.
		Nudged up on phones: the grid centres its content in 100svh, and
		centring absorbs padding changes, so padding alone can never lift
		this text — the translate does. Cleared on desktop, where the
		panel centres the grid instead. */
	.hero-copy {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		text-align: center;
		transform: translateY(-36px);
	}

	@media (width >= 64rem) {
		.hero-grid {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 25rem);
			justify-items: stretch;
			min-height: 0;
			align-content: normal;
			/*
				PADDING EQUALITY, holds at every viewport height. The panel
				centres the grid, so the viewport's leftover splits evenly
				and the fixed paddings decide whether the two sides read
				the same. Above the copy: padding-top (2.5rem) plus the
				greeting's line-box lead (~1.1rem). Below the plate:
				padding-bottom minus the plate's 3rem overhang (4rem −
				3rem = 1rem), plus the dip correction's 1.5rem of lifted
				ink (see .hero-plate's lg block) and the strip's own
				1.5rem before "Locate Us" — both sides read level at any
				window height. 4rem also keeps the plate inside the panel
				(4 > 3rem overhang), so the strip's padding always clears
				it. The grown grid shrinks the centred leftover, which
				rides the content up — the brief's "a little above".
			*/
			padding-top: 2.5rem;
			padding-bottom: 4rem;
			width: 100%;
		}

		.hero-copy {
			align-items: flex-start;
			text-align: left;
			transform: none;
		}

		/*
			The panel stretches to fill the viewport column and centres the
			grid inside it, so the strip below lands on the fold.
		*/
		.hero-panel {
			flex: 1 0 auto;
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
			justify-content: center;
		}
	}

	/*
		The tablet band (md, below lg) flows instead of stretching. The
		one-screen spread exists so Locate / Nights / Reservation can end
		the first screen — but at these widths the strip already sits past
		the fold, so the stretch only pools dead ember under the plate
		(86px of it) and the hero reads bottom-heavy. Here the grid flows,
		the phone lift is unnecessary (nothing centres against it), and
		the content takes equal measured padding above and below:
		header → greeting ink ≈ 46px, plate → border → "Locate Us"
		≈ 47px. Phones keep the centred one-screen shape above untouched;
		lg keeps its own panel-centred balance below.
	*/
	@media (width >= 48rem) and (width < 64rem) {
		.hero-grid {
			min-height: 0;
			align-content: start;
			padding-top: 1.75rem;
			padding-bottom: 1.75rem;
		}

		.hero-copy {
			transform: none;
		}
	}

	/*
		The lede — three lines on phones, two on desktop. Line one is the
		pillars on their own line via the authored <br>; line two runs
		~31em in Plus Jakarta Sans (advance widths off the font file), so
		at 1rem it wraps into two lines in a phone column and holds one
		line in the ~515px desktop column. Stays below the site-wide lede
		token (1.0625rem) so it supports the greeting instead of
		competing with it. Phones get 14px; desktop steps up to 1rem,
		where line two still holds a single line.
	*/
	.hero-lede {
		font-size: 0.875rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}

	@media (width >= 64rem) {
		.hero-lede {
			font-size: 1rem;
		}
	}

	/*
		The doorway — the real logo, shown big. Stacked caps on phones:
		gutter width, an absolute 18rem, and the viewport height (`40svh`
		is a ~50svh-tall plate at 4:5, bezel on top), so the sign stays
		grand without pushing the copy off the first screen.

		At lg it fills its 25rem column — wider than the flat copy column
		by half again — and carries the overhang, so the doorway sets the
		panel's height and the sign reads first.
	*/
	.hero-plate {
		width: min(84vw, 18rem, 40svh);
		margin-inline: auto;
		/* Own stacking context: the glow inside carries z-index: -1, and
			without this it would fall behind .hero-panel's backgrounds.
			A transform (phones ride −12px; lg carries the dip
			correction) creates a context too — the isolate guarantees
			one regardless. The glow has to paint *inside* the doorway,
			never under the panel's ember. */
		isolation: isolate;
		/* Phones: the plate rides up with the text. Cleared on desktop,
			where the overhang margin owns the plate's position. */
		transform: translateY(-12px);
	}

	@media (width >= 64rem) {
		/*
			THE DIP, CANCELLED. A grid item centres on its margin box, so
			this -3rem bottom margin drags the doorway's visual centre
			1.5rem (24px) below the row's centreline — beside the copy it
			read as the photo hanging low. The translate is the exact
			geometric correction: the ink lifts 24px while the layout box
			stays put, so nothing downstream moves and no new protrusion
			past the panel can appear (the plate moves away from the
			border). The strip's padding is retuned to 1.5rem in step so
			the air above and below the hero stays level (see the
			.info-strip lg block).
		*/
		.hero-plate {
			width: 100%;
			margin-inline: 0;
			margin-bottom: -3rem;
			transform: translateY(-1.5rem);
		}
	}

	/*
		The arrival. One rise, 18px, 760ms on the soft curve, `both` so the
		prefers-reduced-motion shortening lands on a finished frame. Delays
		come from the inline --hero-delay set in the markup (greeting →
		lede → action → doorway), which keyframes stagger by hand as a
		sequence rather than by a shared interval — an arrival, not a ticker.
	*/
	.hero-arrive {
		animation: hero-rise 760ms var(--ease-out-soft) both;
		animation-delay: var(--hero-delay, 0ms);
	}

	@keyframes hero-rise {
		from {
			opacity: 0;
			transform: translateY(18px);
		}
		to {
			opacity: 1;
			transform: none;
		}
	}

	/*
		Lamplight behind the arch. A warm oval, painted once and then
	breathing ±25% of its own (already faint) opacity over 9 seconds —
	slow enough to read as a candle, not as animation. Sits behind the
	photograph, visible only at the arch's cut corners; the Photo shell
	is opaque, so it cannot halo into the image.
	*/
	.hero-glow {
		position: absolute;
		inset: -16% -14%;
		z-index: -1;
		pointer-events: none;
		opacity: 0.35;
		background: radial-gradient(
			50% 42% at 50% 34%,
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-canyon-amber) 28%, transparent),
			transparent 70%
		);
		animation: glow-breathe 9s ease-in-out 1.4s infinite alternate;
	}

	@keyframes glow-breathe {
		from {
			opacity: 0.35;
		}
		to {
			opacity: 0.55;
		}
	}

	/*
		The headline — compact on phones, full display on desktop.

		Phone: one line, always. "Welcome to Canyon!" measures ~10.5em in
		Fraunces (advance widths off the font file); the tracking is
		pulled to -0.03em to buy room, bringing it to ~9.95em, so at
		8.5vw a 390px phone sets ~33px (~330px of type against a ~342px
		column). This is the absolute ceiling — smaller phones sit nearly
		edge to edge, with only kerning slack to spare. The 1.5rem floor
		keeps a
		320px viewport inside its ~272px column. `nowrap` holds the line;
		the spans stay inline so the space between them is the only
		break — which never takes.
	*/
	.hero-title {
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 8.5vw, 3rem);
		font-weight: 560;
		line-height: 1.1;
		letter-spacing: -0.03em;
		color: var(--color-canyon-champagne);
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.hero-title .hero-line {
		display: inline;
	}

	@media (width >= 64rem) {
		/* Desktop: the original full-size greeting, "Welcome to" over
			"Canyon!" — the spans stack so the break holds at any viewport
			height, not just when the column forces it. */
		.hero-title {
			font-size: var(--text-hero);
			line-height: 1.02;
			letter-spacing: -0.015em;
			white-space: normal;
			text-wrap: balance;
		}

		.hero-title .hero-line {
			display: block;
		}
	}

	/* ── The strip ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

	/* 30px between the panel's dividing line and the strip on phones.
		Desktop keeps its clearance in the lg block, fixed — a svh-scaled
		value here is what let the hero's vertical balance drift between
		window sizes. */
	.info-strip {
		padding-top: 30px;
		padding-bottom: clamp(1.5rem, 3svh, 2.5rem);
	}

	@media (width >= 64rem) {
		/*
			Fixed 1.5rem, retuned in step with the plate's dip correction:
			the lifted doorway leaves 24px more air before the border, so
			it comes back off here — the air above the hero and below it
			to "Locate Us" reads level at every window height (see the
			.hero-grid lg block for the full identity). Pinned to the
			fold: 10px of landing under Locate / Nights / Reservation.
		*/
		.info-strip {
			margin-top: auto;
			padding-top: 1.5rem;
			padding-bottom: 10px;
		}
	}

	.strip {
		display: grid;
		gap: 2rem;
	}

	@media (width >= 48rem) {
		.strip {
			/* The reference's own proportions: the address is the long one. */
			grid-template-columns: 1.2fr 1fr 1fr;
			gap: 2.75rem;
		}
	}

	/* Icon left, title + info stacked right: the chip centres against the
		whole block, not just the title. Zero gap on phones so each title
		hugs its info; the roomier rhythm returns once the strip runs
		three columns. */
	.strip-item {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 1rem;
	}

	.strip-text {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0;
	}

	@media (width >= 48rem) {
		.strip-text {
			gap: 0.5rem;
		}

		.strip-value {
			margin-top: 0;
		}
	}

	.strip-title {
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
		color: var(--color-canyon-champagne);
	}

	/* The double-bezel, at 48px: amber line icon in a dark disc. */
	.strip-chip {
		display: grid;
		place-items: center;
		width: 3rem;
		height: 3rem;
		flex-shrink: 0;
		border-radius: 9999px;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-canyon-coal);
		background-color: var(--color-canyon-void);
		color: var(--color-canyon-amber);
	}

	/* Beside the chip, under the title — the column geometry places it,
		so no indent is needed at any width. Hugged tight on phones. */
	.strip-value {
		margin: -0.25rem 0 0 0;
		font-size: 0.875rem;
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-canyon-champagne) 80%, transparent);
	}

	.strip-number {
		font-weight: 500;
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		white-space: nowrap;
		color: var(--color-canyon-champagne);
		text-decoration: none;
		transition: color 300ms var(--ease-out-soft);
		/* 44px tap height with no layout shift: the padding grows the
		   hit area, the negative margin gives the space back. */
		display: inline-block;
		padding-block: 0.75rem;
		margin-block: -0.75rem;
	}

	.strip-number:hover {
		color: var(--color-canyon-amber);
	}
</style>
